'''Shinkai Sansha Shrine''' (also simply called " '''Shinkai Shrine''' ") is {{ill|Shinto shrine|a shrine|en}} located in {{ill|Nagano Prefecture|Miyashiro|en}} , {{ill|10=ja|11=田口村_(長野県)|Taguchi|en}}, {{ill|10=ja|11=佐久市|Saku, Nagano|en}}. It was a former {{ill|Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|lt=prefectural shrine|prefectural shrine|en}} and {{ill|Sōja shrine|the main shrine|en}} of {{ill|10=ja|11=佐久郡|Saku District, Shinano|lt=Saku District|en}} . The {{ill|10=ja|11=祭神|enshrined deities|en}} are {{ill|10=ja|11=意岐萩命|Okihagi no Mikoto|en}}, {{ill|10=ja|11=タケミナカタ|Takeminakata|lt=Takeminakata no Mikoto|en}}, {{ill|Kotoshironushi|lt=Kotoshironushi no Mikoto|en}}, and {{ill|Emperor Ōjin|Homudawake no Mikoto|en}} . {{ill|10=ja|11=家紋|Mon (emblem)|lt=The sacred crest|en}} is {{ill|10=ja|11=梶葉|the Kaji leaf|en}} . The founding date is unknown.

== Overview ==
The main deity, {{ill|10=ja|11=興波岐命|Okihagi|en}} no Mikoto, is the grandson of {{ill|Ōkuninushi|Izumo no Okuninushi|en}} no Okami and the god of development of Saku. The father of Okihagi no Mikoto is {{ill|10=ja|11=タケミナカタ|Takeminakata|en}} no Mikoto of {{ill|10=ja|11=諏訪地域|Suwa|en}}, and his mother is {{ill|10=ja|11=上野貫前女神|Ueno Nukisaki no Megami|en}} ( {{ill|10=ja|11=荒船大明神|Arafune Daimyojin|en}} ). Additionally, there is a group of ancient tombs known as the " {{ill|10=ja|11=四十八塚|48zuka|en}} " within the grounds and nearby, which some say are vestiges of ancient beliefs. Shinkai Sansha Shrine, which is said to have been a " '''prefectural shrine''' " during {{ill|Kofun period|the Kofun period|en}}, came under the influence of {{ill|Suwa Taisha|Suwa Shrine|en}} and others during the samurai period, and boasted a sphere of influence spanning a vast area from Saku to {{ill|10=ja|11=上田市|Ueda, Nagano|lt=Ueda|en}} . In addition, Shinkai Sansha Shrine is also known as '''Saku Shrine''' and '''Saku Shrine.'''{{Em dash}}) This is because Okinawa no Mikoto is the ancestral deity of the development of the Saku region. In the " {{ill|Suwa Daimyōjin Ekotoba|En}} " written during {{ill|Nanboku-chō period|the Northern and Southern Courts period|en}}, he is introduced as " '''Nisakunokami''' ."

During {{ill|Sengoku period|the Sengoku period|en}}, {{ill|10=ja|11=武田信玄|Takeda Shingen|en}} is said to have donated a sword to pray for victory in battle and to have the shrine restored; a prayer letter for this has been preserved. <ref name="nagano">[https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1115449/57 県社新海三社神社御神木杉]『[長野県]史蹟名勝天然紀念物調査報告. 第21輯』長野県、1940</ref> During {{ill|Edo period|the Edo period|en}}, a controversy arose over the fact that Shinkai Sansha Shrine was not included in {{ill|Engishiki Jinmyocho|the list of|en}} shrines in Saku, and therefore this ancient shrine, which is the main shrine of Saku, should in fact be included. When {{ill|10=ja|11=神仏分離|Shinbutsu bunri|lt=Shinto and Buddhism were separated|en}} in the early {{ill|Meiji period|en}}, {{ill|10=ja|11=三重塔|the three-story pagoda|en}} of Shinkai Shrine Sansha Shrine (the three-story pagoda of {{ill|10=ja|11=別当寺|Shinkaisan|en}} '''{{ill|10=ja|11=聖徳太子|Prince Shōtoku|en}} {{ill|10=ja|11=本願院|Hongan|en}} -in {{ill|10=ja|11=神宮寺|Jingū-ji|en}}''' which was its head temple) was also scheduled to be destroyed, but when the shrine protested that "this is not a tower but a treasure house," this was accepted, and {{ill|Muromachi period|the Muromachi period|en}} three-story pagoda still stands in the temple grounds today. In addition, {{ill|Shinko-shiki|lt=a sacred procession|en}} was held at Shinkai Sansha Shrine until before the war. This was a major religious ceremony in which a {{ill|10=ja|11=神職|Kannushi|lt=priest|en}} on {{ill|Equestrianism|lt=horseback|en}} traveled from southern and northern Saku areas through the Ogata district . <ref>『佐久の神社と信仰』信濃教育出版部、1989年、261頁。</ref>

== Cultural Assets ==

=== Important Cultural Property (nationally designated) ===

* Three-story pagoda - Built in the late Muromachi period (1515). A three-bay, three-story pagoda with a shingled roof. Designated on August 28, 1907 (Meiji 40). <ref name="#1">『神社考』双葉印刷、1984年、全304頁</ref>
* East Main Shrine - Built in the late Muromachi period (1467-1572). The one-bay, Nagare-zukuri style shrine has a cypress bark roof. Designated on July 29, 1937. <ref name="#1" /> Enshrining the chief deity, Okinawa no Mikoto

== Temple grounds ==

* {{ill|10=ja|11=絹笠神社|Kinugasa Shrine|en}}
* {{ill|10=ja|11=福田稲荷神社|Fukuda Inari Shrine|en}}

In addition, there is a joint hall for 12 eastern and western sub-shrines. Next to the {{ill|Torii|the torii gate|en}} stands the largest tree in the shrine grounds, a large zelkova tree said to be over 1,000 years old. <ref name="nagano">[https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1115449/57 県社新海三社神社御神木杉]『[長野県]史蹟名勝天然紀念物調査報告. 第21輯』長野県、1940</ref>

== Sacred Tree ==
[[File:Shinkai_Sansha_Jinja_map.png|thumb| The sacred cedar tree and the zelkova tree near the torii gate]]
There is a {{ill|Shinboku|There is a sacred|en}} {{ill|10=ja|11=スギ|Cryptomeria|en}} tree about 20 meters in front of the east main hall. Its age is unknown, but as of 1940 it was over 30 metres tall, 12 metres thick at the base, and 8 metres at its diameter. <ref name="nagano">[https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1115449/57 県社新海三社神社御神木杉]『[長野県]史蹟名勝天然紀念物調査報告. 第21輯』長野県、1940</ref> When {{ill|10=ja|11=金剛力士|Nio (Buddhism)|lt=the Niomon Gate|en}} burned down in the early Meiji period, the fire spread to the base of the temple, and all {{ill|Ujigami|the parishioners|en}} gathered together and spent several days extinguishing the fire. Before the war, when the trunk of the tree began to lean severely, they received guidance from Dr. {{ill|10=ja|11=本多静六|Honda Seiroku|en}}, a doctor of {{ill|10=ja|11=博士（林学）|forestry|en}} and had it reinforced with donations from all the parishioners. <ref name="nagano" />

== Anecdote ==

* The road taken during the divine procession was called "Shinkaido" and {{ill|Shinko-shiki|the divine procession|en}} itself was called " {{ill|10=ja|11=御幸|Miyuki|en}} " <ref>『限定復刻版 佐久口碑伝説集 北佐久篇』7ページ。</ref> .
* Behind the shrine is Yoshiike Pond. Long ago, a monk learned of this pond after hearing a prophecy from a snake. It is said that when a monk ground ink in the water of the pond and wrote Sanskrit characters, the characters were reflected on 36 sheets of paper below, and so the Goshintomi Festival began. It was also said that anyone who saw a Shinto priest wearing a white mask going to fetch water in the middle of the night would suffer misfortune, so villagers would go to bed early. <ref>『限定復刻版 佐久口碑伝説集 南佐久篇』121ページ。</ref>
* {{ill|10=ja|11=佐久市長|Mayor of Saku City|en}}, {{ill|10=ja|11=柳田清二|Seiji Yanagida|en}}, spoke about the anime film " {{ill|Your Name|Your Name."|en}} Since it has been introduced as a sacred place for the movie, some people visit the area to verify the location or make a pilgrimage there. <ref>[https://twitter.com/Seiji_Ya/status/771939265534132224 【君の名は。『聖地』新海三社神社】]佐久市長柳田清二Twitter, 2016.9.3</ref>

